{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="Orchestration" heading="Run it now, everywhere" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    Run Puppet, tasks and plans on the nodes you pick &mdash; now or on a
    schedule &mdash; and watch the results come back one node at a time.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Results per node, not per job</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Every target is recorded on its own: status, exit code and output.
      A job that worked on nine nodes and failed on one says exactly that,
      instead of collapsing to a single verdict you then have to go and
      investigate.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      Where a job caused a Puppet run, its result links straight to the
      report that run produced &mdash; so "what did this actually change"
      is one click away.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "jobs-list"}}

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Tasks, plans and schedules</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Run a task from your deployed code, with its parameters checked
      against the types it declares and sensitive values kept out of every
      record. Chain Puppet runs and tasks into a plan you keep and run
      again. Or write a plan in YAML beside your code, with typed
      parameters and results passed from step to step, and the console runs
      it &mdash; no Bolt needed.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      Save any of them to start later, once or repeatedly. A schedule picks
      its nodes each time it fires, and runs with the access its owner has
      at that moment, so a schedule stops doing what its owner may no
      longer do.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Choose targets by rule, not by list</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Name nodes, point at a node group, or write a PQL query, and the
      console works out the nodes when the job starts. Run Puppet as a
      no-op to see what it would change, against another environment, or
      with debug output &mdash; per run.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      Hosts that cannot run the agent can still be reached over SSH. Tasks
      run on them like on any node.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-timeline>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Job created" data-i18n-attr="heading">Targets recorded, each one pending.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Dispatched" data-i18n-attr="heading">Work goes out to the nodes.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Results arrive" data-i18n-attr="heading">Each node reports back as it finishes.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Job completed" data-i18n-attr="heading">The job takes the status its targets add up to.</vox-timeline-item>
</vox-timeline>

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Built to keep up" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n>
    Orchestration runs over NATS, so reaching a large fleet is a matter of
    adding capacity rather than reworking how messages are routed.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
